        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(cron): honor AbortSignal in waitForActiveCronTaskRuns poll",
        "body": "## Summary\nAdd optional `AbortSignal` parameter to `waitForActiveCronTaskRuns` so callers can cancel the drain poll instead of blocking until the deadline expires.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n`waitForActiveCronTaskRuns` in `src/cron/service/active-run-cancellation.ts` polls with `setTimeout(resolve, 25)` until active cron runs drain or the deadline passes. The polling loop does not check any abort signal — a caller that wants to cancel the wait (e.g. a second restart signal arriving during the drain) has no way to stop the loop early. The function always blocks for up to `timeoutMs`, even when the caller already knows the result doesn't matter.\n\n**Code evidence**: [active-run-cancellation.ts:109-111](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/main/src/cron/service/active-run-cancellation.ts#L109-L111) — bare `setTimeout(resolve, …)` in `new Promise` without `AbortSignal` awareness.\n\nSame class of bug fixed in [#109805](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/109805) (auto-reply transient-HTTP retry backoff) and [#109913](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/109913) (discord 429 retry backoff).\n\n## Root Cause\nThe polling loop in `waitForActiveCronTaskRuns` uses a bare `setTimeout`-based sleep with no connection to the caller's lifecycle. If the caller's operation is cancelled mid-wait, the function still blocks.\n\n## Why This Fix\nAdd optional `signal?: AbortSignal` parameter. When the signal fires, clear the timer and resolve immediately — the function returns the current drain state (`{ drained: false, active: N }`) which the caller already handles gracefully. Resolving (not rejecting) preserves backward compatibility: existing callers ignore the result when `drained` is false.\n\nPattern from #109805:\n```typescript\n// Before: bare setTimeout — ignores abort\nawait new Promise<void>((resolve) => { setTimeout(resolve, ms); });\n// After: honors abort signal\nawait new Promise<void>((resolve) => {\n  const timer = setTimeout(resolve, ms);\n  signal?.addEventListener(\"abort\", () => { clearTimeout(timer); resolve(); }, { once: true });\n});\n```\n\n## User Impact\n- **Before**: Gateway restart drain always waits the full 1-second `waitForActiveCronTaskRuns` timeout even when the restart is superseded.\n- **After**: A second abort signal during drain returns near-instantly, speeding up restart churn.\n\n## Evidence\n- `waitForActiveCronTaskRuns` with real `AbortController` and wall-clock timers (no `vi.mock`, no fake timers)\n- Before: abort at 100ms → function still waits ~10000ms (the full timeout) → **FAIL**\n- After: abort at 100ms → function returns ~100ms → **PASS**\n\n## Real Behavior Proof\n\n### Before (on main, bare `setTimeout`)\n\n```bash\n$ node --import tsx scripts/proof-cron-drain-abort.mjs\nresult: drained=false active=1\nelapsed: 10005ms (timeout=10000ms, aborted@100ms)\nFAIL: waited 10005ms despite abort at 100ms — signal was ignored\n```\n\n### After (with fix)\n\n```bash\n$ node --import tsx scripts/proof-cron-drain-abort.mjs\nresult: drained=false active=1\nelapsed: 101ms (timeout=10000ms, aborted@100ms)\nPASS: aborted signal returned near-instantly\n```\n\n## Tests and Validation\n- `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/cron/service/active-run-cancellation.test.ts` — 6/6 passed (3 existing + 3 new)\n- `git diff --check` clean\n",
        "branch": "fix/openai-image-gen-body-idle-timeout",
